WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.475
Block three.

2
00:00:01.475 --> 00:00:04.875
Pretraining changes biological prediction.

3
00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:10.250
On the page, this block is the section "Pretraining changes biological prediction".

4
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:13.000
Blocks one and two built machinery.

5
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:22.325
This block measures what it is worth, on the task we have been using all session, and it contains the result I would most like you to remember from Session eleven.

6
00:00:22.325 --> 00:00:33.750
We follow SignalP through thirty years, see what the language model changed in its latest version, and then run the comparison ourselves on our own four thousand nine hundred and ten proteins.

7
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:41.525
SignalP has been online since nineteen ninety-five and is one of the most used tools in all of bioinformatics.

8
00:00:41.525 --> 00:00:45.900
Its history is, by coincidence, a tour of this entire course.

9
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:51.650
Version one, nineteen ninety-seven: feed-forward neural networks on sliding windows.

10
00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:54.875
That is Session seven, and we covered it there.

11
00:00:54.875 --> 00:00:59.800
Versions two and three added hidden Markov models alongside the networks.

12
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:06.750
That is Session five's machinery, modelling the signal peptide as a series of states with transitions between them.

13
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:16.450
Version five, twenty nineteen: a deep recurrent network, the bidirectional LSTM with a conditional random field, which is Block one of today.

14
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:22.275
Version six, twenty twenty-two: a pretrained protein language model, which is Block two.

15
00:01:22.275 --> 00:01:26.525
Four architectures, thirty years, one biological problem.

16
00:01:26.525 --> 00:01:29.975
And I think there is something worth noticing in that.

17
00:01:29.975 --> 00:01:32.300
The problem did not change.

18
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.575
The signal peptide is the same object Blobel described.

19
00:01:36.575 --> 00:01:42.850
What changed is entirely on our side: what we were able to compute, and what we were able to learn from.

20
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.575
So what did the language model actually buy, in version six?

21
00:01:47.575 --> 00:01:50.300
Three things, and they are specific.

22
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.125
First, rare types became predictable for the first time.

23
00:01:54.125 --> 00:02:01.175
Signal peptides come in several types, handled by different translocation machinery, and some of them are rare.

24
00:02:01.175 --> 00:02:07.075
Two of those types had thirty-six and a hundred and thirteen training sequences respectively.

25
00:02:07.075 --> 00:02:12.275
That is not enough to train a deep network from scratch in any conventional sense.

26
00:02:12.275 --> 00:02:16.750
With a pretrained language model underneath, they became predictable.

27
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:23.250
The authors' own summary is that this confirms the importance of language models for low-data problems.

28
00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:26.975
Second, the organism input was no longer needed.

29
00:02:26.975 --> 00:02:36.975
Earlier versions had to be told what kind of organism the protein came from, because signal peptides differ between, say, Gram-positive bacteria and eukaryotes.

30
00:02:36.975 --> 00:02:46.275
Version six infers that from the sequence itself, because the language model's representation already encodes the taxonomic flavour of a sequence.

31
00:02:46.275 --> 00:02:55.675
Which has a very practical consequence: SignalP six works on metagenomic proteins, where you often do not know what organism they came from at all.

32
00:02:55.675 --> 00:03:00.650
Third, and this is the Session eight connection: where the two versions differed.

33
00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:06.000
On close homologs of the training data, versions five and six were about equal.

34
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.675
The gain appeared below sixty per cent identity.

35
00:03:09.675 --> 00:03:16.700
Version six is better exactly where there is no close relative to lean on, which is where you actually need a predictor.

36
00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.800
One honest footnote the authors include.

37
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:30.375
They redid the data partitioning with a tool called GraphPart to make the splits more rigorous, and even so they could not get below thirty per cent identity between folds.

38
00:03:30.375 --> 00:03:32.975
Perfect separation is hard.

39
00:03:33.575 --> 00:03:38.750
Now our own experiment, which is a simplified version of that comparison.

40
00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:45.475
The question is now per protein rather than per residue: does this protein have a signal peptide at all?

41
00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:52.075
Same four thousand nine hundred and ten proteins, first seventy residues, same homology-aware split.

42
00:03:52.075 --> 00:03:54.125
Three classifiers.

43
00:03:54.125 --> 00:03:58.275
One: logistic regression on one-hot encoded sequence.

44
00:03:58.275 --> 00:04:00.450
The simplest baseline.

45
00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:06.550
Two: a convolutional network on one-hot encoded sequence, which is Session ten applied here.

46
00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:11.050
Three: logistic regression on frozen ESM-2 embeddings.

47
00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:21.925
The residue vectors from ESM-2 are averaged into four hundred and eighty numbers per protein, the language model is kept frozen, and only the logistic regression is trained.

48
00:04:21.925 --> 00:04:26.350
Notice that the third model is the simplest classifier of the three.

49
00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:31.300
All the capability is in the representation, not in the model on top.

50
00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:37.550
That is Session ten's transfer learning exactly: freeze the pretrained thing, train a new last layer.

51
00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:47.575
We use the thirty-five-million-parameter ESM-2, standing in for the larger ProtBERT that SignalP six uses, because it has to run in a lab notebook.

52
00:04:48.175 --> 00:04:51.450
The results, on the homology-aware test set.

53
00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:56.600
Logistic regression on one-hot: MCC nought point seven four five.

54
00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:01.025
Convolutional network on one-hot: nought point eight two five.

55
00:05:01.025 --> 00:05:07.025
Logistic regression on frozen ESM-2 embeddings: nought point nine five five.

56
00:05:07.025 --> 00:05:13.550
In accuracy: nought point eight seven nine, nought point nine one six, and nought point nine seven eight.

57
00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:21.700
So the simplest classifier, on top of a pretrained representation, beats a purpose-built convolutional network by a wide margin.

58
00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:27.175
I want to be precise about where the credit goes, because it is easy to say this badly.

59
00:05:27.175 --> 00:05:30.575
The logistic regression did not become clever.

60
00:05:30.575 --> 00:05:34.000
It is the same weighted sum it has always been.

61
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:48.375
What changed is what it was given to work with: four hundred and eighty numbers per protein that already encode the context of every residue, produced by a model that learned from millions of sequences without ever seeing a signal peptide label.

62
00:05:48.375 --> 00:05:56.025
And that is Session seven's lesson, in its strongest form yet: the representation of the input matters as much as the model.

63
00:05:56.025 --> 00:06:00.550
In Session seven it was profiles against single sequences.

64
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:04.400
Here it is a pretrained language model against one-hot.

65
00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:07.200
Same lesson, thirty years apart.

66
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:12.675
And here is the result that I think is the most important one in this session.

67
00:06:12.675 --> 00:06:17.300
We repeat the comparison with artificially restricted training data.

68
00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:23.700
With twenty labelled training proteins: ESM-2 reaches MCC nought point seven six two.

69
00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:30.800
One-hot linear reaches nought point zero four five, and the one-hot convolutional network nought point one two two.

70
00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.875
Twenty proteins.

71
00:06:32.875 --> 00:06:36.825
The one-hot models have essentially nothing; they are at chance.

72
00:06:36.825 --> 00:06:40.525
The pretrained one is already at nought point seven six.

73
00:06:40.525 --> 00:06:43.550
Now the comparison that states it best.

74
00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:48.975
Fifty labelled proteins with ESM-2 gives nought point nine zero seven.

75
00:06:48.975 --> 00:06:55.700
Four thousand one hundred and sixty-nine labelled proteins with one-hot gives nought point seven four five.

76
00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:58.175
Fifty beats four thousand.

77
00:06:58.175 --> 00:07:01.775
Think about what that means for how you would plan a project.

78
00:07:01.775 --> 00:07:12.450
If your task has a few dozen labels and you were planning to spend a year generating a few thousand more, you should first check what a frozen pretrained model does with the few dozen you have.

79
00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:16.625
It may be better than the year of work, and it costs an afternoon.

80
00:07:16.625 --> 00:07:23.900
And the way to understand why is on the slide: the labels are not teaching the model what a signal peptide is from scratch.

81
00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:31.350
The model already knows what hydrophobic N-terminal regions look like, because predicting masked residues forced it to.

82
00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:37.225
The labels only point at which of the things it already knows is the thing we are asking about.

83
00:07:37.225 --> 00:07:50.275
With all four thousand one hundred and sixty-nine training proteins, for completeness: one-hot nought point seven four five, one-hot convolutional nought point eight four five, ESM-2 nought point nine five five.

84
00:07:50.875 --> 00:07:55.750
One more decomposition, and it is the methodological heart of the session.

85
00:07:55.750 --> 00:08:01.075
Split the test set by how similar each protein is to the closest thing in training.

86
00:08:01.075 --> 00:08:05.575
Above ninety per cent identity: every model is perfect.

87
00:08:05.575 --> 00:08:11.625
If a near-identical protein was in the training set, anything works, including looking it up.

88
00:08:11.625 --> 00:08:18.825
Below thirty per cent identity: the one-hot models get between nought point six eight and nought point seven seven.

89
00:08:18.825 --> 00:08:22.150
ESM-2 gets nought point nine six.

90
00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:31.975
That is the same pattern as SignalP six against SignalP five: the two are equal on close homologs, and pretraining wins where there is no relative to lean on.

91
00:08:31.975 --> 00:08:44.700
And look at where the test set actually lives: four hundred and forty-one of the nine hundred and eighty-two test proteins fall in that lowest bin, and thirty-seven per cent of the test set has no detectable homolog at all.

92
00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:49.150
So the hard bin is not an edge case; it is most of the problem.

93
00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:53.250
Which is why a random split would be a diagnostic at best.

94
00:08:53.250 --> 00:09:00.175
On a random split you would be reporting mostly the top bin, where everything is perfect and nothing is distinguished.

95
00:09:00.175 --> 00:09:06.900
Three caveats, because the comparison is not perfectly clean and you should hear it from me rather than discover it.

96
00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:15.275
Our task, does this protein have a signal peptide, is easier than SignalP's full task of also finding the cleavage site and the type.

97
00:09:15.275 --> 00:09:19.675
Our ESM-2 is small and frozen rather than fine-tuned.

98
00:09:19.675 --> 00:09:28.625
And ESM-2 did see these sequences during pretraining, though never their labels, exactly as ProtBERT did inside SignalP six.

99
00:09:29.225 --> 00:09:33.825
Before the summary, where this is going, and it is further than you might think.

100
00:09:33.825 --> 00:09:40.950
ESM3 has ninety-eight billion parameters and was trained on sequence, structure and function together.

101
00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:44.800
It was prompted to generate new fluorescent proteins.

102
00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:48.975
One of them, which they call esmGFP, actually glows.

103
00:09:48.975 --> 00:10:01.150
And here is the number that makes it remarkable: it is only thirty-six per cent identical to the jellyfish GFP, and fifty-eight per cent identical to the closest known fluorescent protein of any kind.

104
00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:08.200
The authors estimate that this distance corresponds to more than five hundred million years of natural evolution.

105
00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:18.400
So a model trained to predict sequences produced a working protein that evolution has not produced, and that is a different kind of claim from predicting an annotation.

106
00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:28.900
Evo 2 does the same thing for genomes: forty billion parameters, about nine trillion bases from all domains of life, a context window of a million bases.

107
00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:39.750
It predicts the effects of mutations, including clinically relevant BRCA1 variants, without being fine-tuned for that, and it generates sequences at genome scale.

108
00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:46.250
And it is fully open, including its training data, which matters for the reason Session nine ended on.

109
00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:56.050
These same models are the building blocks of the virtual-cell projects: SciLifeLab's Alpha Cell from Session ten, and the Biohub's Virtual Biology Initiative.

110
00:10:56.050 --> 00:11:01.700
And then the question on the slide, which is a genuine one and which I will not resolve for you.

111
00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:07.525
Does a protein language model understand biology, or does it only model sequences?

112
00:11:07.525 --> 00:11:19.850
Here is the case for taking it seriously: a model that designs a working protein five hundred million years of evolution away from anything known has learned something real about what makes a protein a protein.

113
00:11:19.850 --> 00:11:27.225
Whether the word understanding is the right one is exactly the argument, and it is worth having carefully rather than loudly.

114
00:11:27.825 --> 00:11:31.075
The summary, back to the slide we started on.

115
00:11:31.075 --> 00:11:33.750
Three ways to use context.

116
00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:38.975
A convolutional network: local context, growing layer by layer with depth.

117
00:11:38.975 --> 00:11:43.850
A recurrent network: context carried along the sequence step by step.

118
00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:48.175
Attention: any position to any other, directly, in one step.

119
00:11:48.175 --> 00:11:51.175
Two ways to learn a representation.

120
00:11:51.175 --> 00:11:58.375
A supervised model learns its representation from your labels, so it can only learn as much as your labels contain.

121
00:11:58.375 --> 00:12:08.475
A pretrained language model learns its representation first, from millions of unlabelled sequences, so that a handful of labels can go an extraordinarily long way.

122
00:12:08.475 --> 00:12:12.100
Fifty against four thousand one hundred and sixty-nine.

123
00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:18.575
And here is the exercise I would like you to do, out loud if you are in the room and in your head if you are not.

124
00:12:18.575 --> 00:12:22.700
For each of the three architectures, name one thing it cannot do.

125
00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:27.975
The convolutional network cannot reach far along the sequence without becoming deep.

126
00:12:27.975 --> 00:12:36.350
The forward recurrent network cannot see the future, which on signal peptides cost it seventy-six per cent of the first five residues.

127
00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:41.025
And attention, on its own, has no idea what order anything is in.

128
00:12:41.025 --> 00:12:47.875
Every architecture is a set of assumptions, and knowing which assumption each one makes is how you choose between them.

129
00:12:48.475 --> 00:12:50.800
That is Session eleven.

130
00:12:50.800 --> 00:13:03.425
Session twelve is the last one, and it goes from predicting a label about a protein to predicting the protein itself: AlphaFold, and then the generative models that design proteins rather than describe them.

131
00:13:03.425 --> 00:13:06.175
Everything we have built arrives there.

132
00:13:06.175 --> 00:13:17.450
Session five's multiple alignments, Session ten's residual convolutional networks on contact maps, and today's attention, all in one system that solved a fifty-year-old problem.

133
00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:26.275
The Session eleven page has all of this written out, and its "Check your understanding" section at the end is the fastest way to find out whether it stuck.

134
00:13:26.275 --> 00:13:30.325
The course notebook is linked in the box under the page's title.

135
00:13:30.325 --> 00:13:31.950
See you there.

